  "source_document": {
    "document_id": "2605a658-a5b2-4feb-8941-ca7139d69df3",
    "origin_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/trucker-built-directory-tops-12000-listings-as-roadside-repair-network-expands",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-24T16:35:05.674660+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/trucker-built-directory-tops-12000-listings-as-roadside-repair-network-expands",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/trucker-built-directory-tops-12000-listings-as-roadside-repair-network-expands",
    "origin_publisher": "FreightWaves",
    "published_at": "2026-11-24T14:00:00+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-24T14:00:00+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-24T16:36:54.634608+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-24T16:36:54.634608+00:00",
    "body": "A free online platform built by a veteran truck driver to help truckers find parking, roadside repairs and other services has exploded to more than 12,000 listings nationwide.\nShawn Gresham , founder of Trucking Life With Shawn , said the directory has grown rapidly since its public launch in October, when it listed 2,031 truck parking locations across 45 states and 259 roadside repair and towing providers.\n“I feel like this thing’s tied to a rocket,” Gresham told FreightWaves. “It’s crazy how this thing is blowing up so fast.”\nThe latest expansion follows an integration with Truck Parking Club , which added thousands of parking locations to Gresham’s directory and allows drivers to access paid parking reservations through the platform.\nThe growth is also generating business for some roadside service providers.\nChris Brown, owner of Brown’s Diesel Repair & Towing in Tunnel Hill, Georgia, said his company has experienced an 15% increase in call volume since joining the directory.\n“We’ve gained probably, I’d say, around 15% call volume,” Brown told FreightWaves. “It’s working. It’s showing up.”\nBrown said customers have specifically mentioned finding his business through Gresham’s website, something he hasn’t consistently experienced with other directories where he advertises.\nDrivers help build roadside repair network\nGresham, a 17-year trucking veteran, said the fastest-growing portion of his platform has been its roadside repair, tire service and heavy-duty towing directory.\nIn a Nov. 12 update, Gresham reported 2,555 repair and roadside service listings, compared with just 259 when FreightWaves first reported on the platform in October.\nThe directory also included 1,946 truck stops, 801 heavy towing companies, 259 weigh stations and 164 CAT scales at that time.\nGresham said many of the repair listings originate with drivers who recommend businesses they have used during breakdowns.\nHe described drivers submitting recommendations for repair shops that responded quickly, provided reliable service or helped them during overnight emergencies.\nRather than automatically publishing those submissions, Gresham said his team checks the business information, reviews public feedback, contacts the provider and verifies the location.\n“We verify the GPS pin to make sure it’s in the right location,” Gresham said. “And then we approve it.”\nHe said the volume of submissions has increased enough that he has hired additional people to help review listings.\nThe platform is designed around interstate exits, allowing drivers to search by state, highway and travel direction to locate repair providers and parking areas.\nGresham said his goal is to eventually have at least one free repair provider listing available at every interstate exit where services exist.\nShawn Gresham, founder of TruckingLifeWithShawn.com, said the directory has grown to more than 12,000 listings of truck parking locations, as well as roadside repair and towing providers, since it launched in October. (Photo: Courtesy)\nGeorgia towing operator reports more business\nBrown, who has worked in towing for 24 years and operated his own business since 2018, said finding trustworthy roadside assistance remains a challenge for truck drivers, particularly independent owner-operators.\nHe said drivers sometimes encounter inexperienced providers, unexpected charges or repair services that cannot perform the work they advertise.\nBrown believes having parking, towing, repair and fuel information available through one website can simplify decisions during breakdowns.\nHe recalled a recent incident involving a truck with a damaged wheel bearing. The driver needed to arrange towing, locate a repair facility and find somewhere to leave the trailer.\nThe driver initially attempted to use several websites to coordinate the arrangements, Brown said.\nAfter Brown recommended Gresham’s directory, the driver was able to locate the necessary services more easily.\n“He said, ‘that was so simple,'” Brown said. “I have four different webpages pulled up trying to find somewhere to park the trailer, somebody to work on it.”\nBrown said the driver was able to coordinate parking and repair arrangements using the directory, illustrating how consolidating information can help when a truck is disabled.\nBrown also said the directory has introduced his company to potential customers who previously might not have found it.\nHe said other towing operators have contacted him to ask whether participating in the platform has been worthwhile.\n“Once I told them that it works, and we’ve picked up call volume, now they’re interested,” Brown said.\nFree for drivers, paid placements for businesses\nGresham said drivers can use the directory without creating an account or paying subscription fees.\nThe website generates revenue through paid featured placements for repair businesses, book sales and affiliate arrangements with Truck Parking Club.\nBusinesses can receive a free basic listing, while companies seeking greater visibility along a highway corridor can purchase expanded placement.\nGresham said featured corridor placements cost $99 per month without a long-term contract.\nBrown’s Diesel Repair & Towing is among the businesses paying for expanded placement along Interstate 75 in Georgia.\nGresham said Brown’s listing covers approximately 60 exits, allowing drivers searching along that stretch of highway to find the business.\nHe emphasized that paid placement does not exempt providers from complaints or negative feedback.\nGresham said businesses receiving repeated complaints about poor service could lose their featured status.\nThe platform’s business model is designed to generate revenue from commercial listings and parking referrals while keeping its core driver tools free, he said.\nTruck Parking Club integration expands directory\nThe latest increase in listings follows an agreement to integrate Truck Parking Club’s parking inventory into the platform.\nGresham said the integration expanded the directory to more than 12,000 listings, including parking locations that drivers can reserve through Truck Parking Club.\nThe integration also allows drivers to identify parking facilities that may be several miles from an interstate exit, rather than only locations immediately adjacent to the highway.\nGresham said he receives affiliate compensation when drivers reserve parking through the arrangement.\nThe expanded inventory is expected to support a truck navigation system and trip planner that Gresham is developing.\nThe GPS system remains in testing with 87 drivers, and Gresham said approximately 7,200 people have joined a waiting list.\nHe is targeting New Year’s Eve for its public release.\nThe planned navigation system will incorporate parking locations, repair providers and trip-planning features into a single application.\nGresham said the Android version has progressed through the Google Play process, while work continues on an Apple version.\nPlatform traffic grows\nGresham said activity on the directory has increased significantly since its launch.\nHe reported that daily clicks have risen from approximately 32 to more than 1,000.\nGresham said around 2,800 people have subscribed to receive diesel price information by email.\nThe website also provides CDL practice tests, educational materials and hours-of-service calculation tools.\nGresham, who is also establishing Trucking Life Academy in Dalton, Georgia, said the school’s opening remains pending final approval from the Georgia Department of Driver Services.\nFor Brown, the directory’s appeal is its ability to help drivers and service providers connect without requiring truckers to search numerous websites during a breakdown.\n“I would refer to it as a one-stop website,” Brown said.\nGresham said the rapid growth has exceeded his initial expectations, but the goal remains to make useful information readily available to working drivers.\n“Drivers are happy. Everybody’s happy,” he said.\nWhy it matters: Shawn Gresham, founder of TruckingLifeWithShawn.com, said his platform is helping connect truckers with parking and roadside assistance while offering independent repair and towing businesses another way to reach customers without charging drivers subscription fees.",
